.TH qmerge "1" "Sep 2026" "Gentoo Foundation" "qmerge"
.SH NAME
qmerge \- fetch and merge binary package
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qmerge
\fI[opts] <pkgnames>\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fIqmerge\fR retrieves Gentoo binary packages from a local \fIPKGDIR\fR
and/or one or more remote binhosts and installs them onto the system,
registering each package in the VDB. It is a binary\-package manager:
it does not build from source.
.P
Both the \fBxpak\fR (\fI.tbz2\fR) and the GLEP\ 78 \fBgpkg\fR
(\fI.gpkg.tar\fR) archive formats are supported, including OpenPGP
signature verification of signed \fBgpkg\fR packages (see \fBqetuto\fR(1)
for keyring setup). Remote catalogues are described by
\fIbinrepos.conf\fR; several binhosts may be consulted, in priority
order.
.P
Package downloads use a built\-in \fBlibcurl\fR fetcher by default, or
the command named in \fIQFETCHCOMMAND\fR when it is set. Fetched binhost
indexes are cached and refreshed subject to their TTL and
If\-Modified\-Since headers.
.P
\fBQFETCH_WRAPPER\fR is the \fBFETCH_WRAPPER\fR of \fBmake.conf\fR(5)
for \fBQFETCHCOMMAND\fR and \fBQRESUMECOMMAND\fR: a command prefix,
most commonly \fBssh\fR(1), that receives the expanded fetch command
as one shell\-quoted argument, so a wrapper that re\-interprets its
arguments through a shell runs   it. When   unset,  portage's
\fBFETCH_WRAPPER\fR applies. Only the command fetch path is wrapped;
the built\-in \fBlibcurl\fR fetcher runs in\-process.
.P
.nf
QFETCH_WRAPPER="ssh \-i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 user@host"
.fi
.P
Dependency resolution follows \fBemerge\fR(1) closely enough for binary
updates: \fB\-u\fR, \fB\-D\fR and \fB\-N\fR select updates the same way,
sets such as \fB@world\fR and \fB@system\fR are understood, and
\fB\-\-rebuilt\-binaries\fR reinstalls same\-version rebuilds advertised
by a binhost.
.P
\fIqmerge\fR maintains only the local \fIPKGDIR/Packages\fR index (see
\fB\-\-index\fR); remote binhost catalogues are fetched and cached.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-fetch\fR
Fetches the index update (without arguments). The \fIPackages\fR
index of every configured binhost is fetched, together with the
package moves and news it publishes, which are applied. With
arguments: download the named packages and their dependencies into
\fIPKGDIR\fR without merging them, like \fBemerge\ \-f\fR. See
\fB\-\-index\fR for rebuilding the local index from the packages on
disk.
.TP
\fB\-F\fR, \fB\-\-force\fR
Force\-install the named target only: select the newest available
binary package regardless of \fBSLOT\fR, bypassing the mask,
keyword, license, \fB\-\-binpkg\-respect\-use\fR and
\fB\-\-usepkg\-exclude\fR checks, and re\-fetch and re\-index it.
Collision protection remains enforced.  Only the explicitly named
atom is affected; dependency selection is unchanged.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-search\fR
Search all the binhost indexes for packages whose name matches the
given regular expression.  With \fB\-v\fR, entries that cannot be
installed on the current system are listed as well, other
repositories' candidates are shown with the reason they were not
selected, and a match count is printed. With \fB\-vv\fR the raw
search (full details) is shown : every version and every build instance
in every repository with its per\-instance USE, plus instance
counts, regardless of installability.
.TP
\fB\-K\fR, \fB\-\-install\fR
Install (merge) the matched binary package(s) onto the system and
register them in the VDB.  This is the default consuming action,
analogous to \fBemerge\ \-K\fR.
.TP
\fB\-U\fR, \fB\-\-unmerge\fR
Unmerge the named package(s) from the system, running the
shared\-file ownership scan so that files still owned by another
installed package are preserved. The removal, pkg_prerm and
pkg_postrm included, runs under portage's vdb lock
(\fIvar/db/.pkg.portage_lockfile\fR under \fBROOT\fR), the one
\fBemerge\fR takes for its own merges and unmerges, so the two
wait for each other. The lock is released and its file removed
when the last named package is gone and the world file updated.
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-erase\fR
Like \fB\-\-unmerge\fR but skip the shared\-file ownership scan:
every recorded file is removed except those under
\fBCONFIG_PROTECT\fR.  Use with care.
.TP
\fB\-W\fR \fI[arg]\fR, \fB\-\-deselect\fR \fI[arg]\fR
Remove the given atoms and \fB@sets\fR from the world favorites
file without touching the installed packages, like
\fBemerge\ \-\-deselect\fR.  Unmerge actions (\fB\-U\fR,
\fB\-e\fR) imply this for the packages they remove; pass
\fB\-\-deselect=n\fR to keep the world entries across an
unmerge.  World entries still satisfied by another installed
version or slot are kept.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-pretend\fR
Show what would be done without making any change to the system,
the VDB or \fIPKGDIR\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-keepwork\fR
Do not remove the unpacked binary packages from the \fIqmerge\fR
temporary directory after merging.  Useful for inspection and
debugging.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-update\fR
Only consider packages that are actually newer than the installed
version, analogous to \fBemerge\ \-u\fR.
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-deep\fR
Consider the whole dependency tree when selecting updates rather
than just the named packages.  Combined with \fB\-u\fR this is
equivalent to \fBemerge\ \-uD\fR.
.TP
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-newuse\fR
Reinstall binary packages whose built \fBUSE\fR flags (including
\fB*_TARGETS\fR expansions) differ from the currently installed
configuration, analogous to \fBemerge\ \-N\fR.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-noreplace\fR
Skip named targets that an installed package already satisfies
instead of reinstalling them, like \fBemerge\ \-n\fR.  The
skipped targets are still recorded in the world favorites file,
so this selects installed packages into \fB@world\fR without
merging anything.
.TP
\fB\-\-rebuilt\-binaries\fR \fI[arg]\fR
Reinstall a binary package of the \fIsame\fR version when the
binhost advertises a newer rebuild of it.  Accepts an optional
\fIy\fR/\fIn\fR argument and is enabled automatically together with
\fB\-D\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-rebuilt\-binaries\-timestamp\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Restrict \fB\-\-rebuilt\-binaries\fR to rebuilds whose
\fBBUILD_TIME\fR is greater than or equal to \fI<arg>\fR (a Unix
epoch), so older rebuilds are ignored.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Don't prompt before overwriting.
.TP
\fB\-1\fR, \fB\-\-oneshot\fR
Do not add the named package(s) to the \fIworld\fR favourites set,
analogous to \fBemerge\ \-\-oneshot\fR.
.TP
\fB\-O\fR, \fB\-\-nodeps\fR
Merge only the named package(s); do not pull in or merge their
dependencies.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-index\fR
Rebuild the local \fIPKGDIR/Packages\fR index from the binary
packages currently on disk, then exit.  The rebuild is incremental
(unchanged entries are kept verbatim) and atomic; \fB\-F\fR forces a
full rebuild.  The compressed \fIPackages.gz\fR sibling is updated
when the \fIcompress\-index\fR feature is enabled.
.TP
\fB\-\-fetchonly\fR
Alias for \fB\-f\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-phases\fR
Print the \fBpkg_*\fR phase functions a binary package would run at
merge time, then exit without merging.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-phases\fR
Bootstrapping. Merge files and record packages in the VDB without
executing any \fBpkg_*\fR phase function, so no shell is required
on the target. Every skipped phase is announced and recorded in
\fIvar/lib/portage/.qmerge\-skipped\-phases\fR under \fBROOT\fR for
a later replay once a shell is available. Intended for
bootstrapping minimal chroots (or containers). user/group creation and
cache\-update work normally done by phases does not happen while
this is active. \fIQMERGE_NO_PHASES=y\fR in make.conf or the
environment enables the same behaviour. If you don't have
\fIbin/bash\fR and \fI/bin/sh\fR , qmerge warns that
the pkg_* phases cannot run.
.TP
\fB\-\-backtrack\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Number of conflict\-repair rounds allowed during dependency
resolution (default 20). A value of 0 disables backtracking.
This  covers both strand repair (pulling rebuilt revdep
binary packages) and same\-slot unification. When a resolution would
install two versions of one package in the same \fBSLOT\fR, the
losing version is masked and resolution restarts, the way portage
backtracks on slot conflicts. A user\-named target is never the
side masked away. \fIQMERGE_SLOT_UNIFY=0\fR in make.conf or the
environment disables the unification rounds, restoring the old
refuse\-on\-first\-collision behaviour.
.TP
\fB\-j\fR \fI<arg>\fR, \fB\-\-jobs\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Number of parallel download jobs: an integer \fIN\fR, \fI0\fR for
one per CPU, \fIy\fR for the maximum, or \fIn\fR for serial
downloads (default 4). Affects fetching only, not merging.
.TP
\fB\-\-usepkg\-exclude\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Never satisfy the given atoms from a binary package.  Unlike
\fB\-\-exclude\fR there is no source fallback: if only a binary
package could satisfy the atom, \fIqmerge\fR refuses rather than
building from source.
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Hold the given atoms at their currently installed version and drop
them from the merge without raising an error, analogous to
\fBemerge\ \-\-exclude\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-usepkg\-exclude\-live\fR
Reject binary packages that were built from a live ebuild
(\fBPROPERTIES=live\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-binpkg\-respect\-use\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Control the \fBUSE\fR check applied to candidate binary packages.
\fIy\fR requires a full \fBUSE\fR match across all repositories;
\fIn\fR disables the check.  The default is a weak check applied to
foreign repositories only.
.TP
\fB\-\-getbinpkg\-exclude\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Never take the given atoms from any binhost (name and slot atoms
only).  Adds to the per\-repository \fIgetbinpkg\-exclude\fR set
from \fIbinrepos.conf\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-getbinpkg\-include\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Only ever take the given atoms from a binhost (a whitelist; name and
slot atoms only).
.TP
\fB\-\-repos\fR
List the configured binhost repositories in priority order, then
exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-list\-set\fR
Print the expanded members of the given set(s) (\fI@world\fR,
\fI@system\fR, \fI@selected\fR, \fI@profile\fR, custom sets from
\fIetc/portage/sets\fR under \fBPORTAGE_CONFIGROOT\fR ,
including nested set references), one atom per line sorted, then
exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-info\fR
Show aprox precise how the matched package was compiled: identity
(\fBSLOT\fR, repository, \fBBUILD_ID\fR, \fBBUILD_TIME\fR), the
recorded build environment (\fBCHOST\fR, \fBCFLAGS\fR,
\fBFEATURES\fR, \fBUSE\fR against \fBIUSE\fR), every dependency
class with its slot-operator bindings frozen at build time
(\fIdev-libs/openssl:0/3=\fR), and the soname layer
(\fBREQUIRES\fR/\fBPROVIDES\fR). Reports the installed package by
default: \fIpkg@binrepo\fR reports the binhost catalog entry, and
an uninstalled atom falls back to the best binhost candidate with
a notice. With \fB\-v\fR each dependency class is additionally
resolved against the VDB under the package's own recorded
\fBUSE\fR, and the per\-object \fBNEEDED.ELF.2\fR records are
printed. Similar in spirit to \fBemerge\ \-\-info\ <atom>\fR but
covers the dependency and soname layers too.
.TP
\fB\-\-exact\fR
With -s: match plain keys as whole names, not substrings.
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\-going\fR \fI[arg]\fR
Continue past a failed package like emerge --keep-going: hide from the list, re-resolve, drop dependents that lost their provider, merge the rest (y/n, default n).
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-depclean\fR
Remove installed packages that nothing in \fI@world\fR needs, like
\fBemerge\ \-\-depclean\fR, with the same algorithm. The keep graph
is resolved over the installed packages alone, \fB||\fR alternatives
are decided the way portage's dep_zapdeps decides them, and the
removal order unmerges each package before its dependencies. With
atoms, only the matching packages are considered and a package
something else still needs is left alone (\fB\-v\fR names who needs
it); the named packages are also deselected from the world file
unless \fB\-\-deselect=n\fR is given. Nothign is removed when a
runtime dependency of a kept package cannot be resolved, or when
\fI@world\fR is empty or refers to a set that does not exist.
\fB\-p\fR prints portage's listing without removing anything.
Like emerge, the built\-in sets (\fI@world\fR, \fI@system\fR,
\fI@selected\fR, \fI@profile\fR) are refused as arguments. Only
file sets from \fIetc/portage/sets\fR and \fI@preserved\-rebuild\fR
are accepted. \fIQMERGE_PROTOCOL_OMEGA=1\fR (make.conf or the
environment) removes the restriction: the built\-in set stops
protecting its atoms, which become the only removal candidates
(everything else stays, orphaned dependencies are left for a
following plain \fB\-\-depclean\fR). In every depclean run, Omega or
not, the packages owning the running qmerge, the installed \fIq\fR
and \fI/bin/sh\fR, the packages providing the libraries those
binaries link against (per the recorded \fBNEEDED.ELF.2\fR data)
and their dependency closure are kept always, shown by \fB\-v\fR as
required by \fI@____qmerge_imasurvivor____\fR;; an Omega run refuses
when no installed package owns them. The Protocol Omega precedes
the listing and the prompts. The calculation holds portage's vdb
lock while the installed packages are read, and the removal holds
it from the first unmerge to the world file update, like
\fB\-\-unmerge\fR. A failed unmerge stops the whole run with its
status.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-prune\fR
Removes all but the highest installed version of a package from your
system. Use \fB\-\-prune\fR together with \fB\-\-verbose\fR to show
reverse dependencies or with \fB\-\-nodeps\fR to ignore all dependencies.
\fBWARNING: This action can remove packages from your world file! Check
the emerge output of the next \-\-depclean run carefully! Use
\-\-depclean to avoid this issue.\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-with\-bdeps\fR \fI<arg>\fR
With \fB\-\-depclean\fR: \fIy\fR (the default, portage's
\fIauto\fR) also follows the \fBDEPEND\fR and \fBBDEPEND\fR of
installed packages when computing what is needed; \fIn\fR follows
only the runtime classes, so packages needed at build time alone
become removable.  \fIQMERGE_WITH_BDEPS\fR in make.conf or the
environment sets the same.
.TP
\fB\-\-depclean\-lib\-check\fR \fI<arg>\fR
With \fB\-\-depclean\fR: \fIy\fR (the default) keeps a package whose
library another kept package still links against, according to the
recorded \fBNEEDED.ELF.2\fR data, reporting portage's "link level
dependencies" notice; \fIn\fR skips that check. Like portage the
check is not run when \fBFEATURES=preserve\-libs\fR is active, since
the preserved\-libs registry keeps such libraries instead.
\fIQMERGE_DEPCLEAN_LIB_CHECK\fR in make.conf or the environment sets
the same.
.TP
\fB\-\-debug\fR
Run the ebuild phase shell functions with \fBset\ \-x\fR for
tracing.
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Set the ROOT env var.
.TP
\fB\-\-overlay\fR \fI<arg>\fR
Select the given overlay for use, instead of all found (see q -o).
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Report full package versions, emit more elaborate output.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Tighter output; suppress warnings.
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-nocolor\fR
Don't output color.
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR
Force color in output.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print this help and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version and exit.

